[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Search Performance Optimization and Caching in Elasticsearch"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As data volumes grow, maintaining low latency and high throughput in Elasticsearch becomes challenging. Optimizing search performance requires a combination of smart query design, effective caching strategies, and appropriate cluster configurations. This tutorial covers the core mechanisms Elasticsearch uses to accelerate searches and how to leverage them."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Caching Layers in Elasticsearch"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Elasticsearch relies on multiple caching layers to reduce disk I/O and speed up execution times. Knowing how these caches work is essential for tuning."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Node Query Cache: Caches the results of queries that are part of a filter context. It uses an LRU (Least Recently Used) eviction policy."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Shard Request Cache: Caches the full search results (hits, aggregations, and suggestions) at the shard level when queries are run with size=0."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Field Data Cache: Stores field values in JVM heap memory when fields are sorted, aggregated, or accessed via scripting. (Note: Doc values are preferred for this to save heap)."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Leveraging the Filter Context for Query Caching"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To benefit from the Node Query Cache, structure your queries using the filter context instead of the query context. Filter contexts are automatically cached by Elasticsearch because they only care whether a document matches, not how well it matches (scoring is ignored)."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"GET /products/_search
{
  "query": {
    "bool": {
      "filter": [
        { "term": { "status": "active" } },
        { "range": { "price": { "gte": 100, "lte": 500 } } }
      ]
    }
  }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring the Shard Request Cache"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The shard request cache is ideal for dashboards where identical queries are executed frequently by multiple users. You can explicitly enable or disable this cache per request, and you can tune its size in the elasticsearch.yml configuration file."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"# elasticsearch.yml configuration
indices.requests.cache.size: "5%""},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Optimizing Mappings and Doc Values"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Proper field mapping significantly impacts performance. Always prefer doc values over field data for sorting, aggregations, and parent-child relationships. Doc values are stored on disk in a column-stride fashion, allowing Elasticsearch to scale memory usage efficiently."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"PUT /logs
{
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "timestamp": {
        "type": "date"
      },
      "status_code": {
        "type": "integer"
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Search Performance"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid expensive operations like wildcard queries at the beginning of terms (e.g., *search)."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pagination at scale: Avoid deep pagination using from and size. Instead, use search_after with a point in time (PIT) API for efficient deep scrolling."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Routing: Use custom routing keys to target specific shards when searching, reducing scatter-gather overhead across the cluster."}]
